]},"inline":[{"kind":"text","text":"Wilson L Taylor. 1953. Cloze procedure: A new tool for measuring readability. Journalism Bulletin, 30(4):415-433."}]},{"id":"folio-987545ffb087f1ece898-p11-b9-2-ii","type":"standoff-editor-block","properties":{"standoffProperties":[]},"inline":[{"kind":"text","text":"Erik F Tjong Kim Sang and Fien De Meulder. 2003. Introduction to the conll-2003 shared task: Language-independent named entity recognition. In CoNLL."}]},{"id":"folio-987545ffb087f1ece898-p11-b9-3-ii","type":"standoff-editor-block","properties":{"standoffProperties":[]},"inline":[{"kind":"text","text":"Joseph Turian, Lev Ratinov, and Yoshua Bengio. 2010."}]},{"id":"folio-987545ffb087f1ece898-p11-b11-0-ii","type":"standoff-editor-block","properties":{"standoffProperties":[]},"inline":[{"kind":"text","text":"Word representations: A simple and general method for semi-supervised learning. In Proceedings of the 48th Annual Meeting of the Association for Compu-tational Linguistics, ACL '10, pages 384-394."}]},{"id":"folio-987545ffb087f1ece898-p11-b11-1-ii","type":"standoff-editor-block","properties":{"standoffProperties":[]},"inline":[{"kind":"text","text":"Ashish Vaswani, Noam Shazeer, Niki Parmar, Jakob Uszkoreit, Llion Jones, Aidan N Gomez, Lukasz Kaiser, and Illia Polosukhin. 2017. Attention is all you need. In Advances in Neural Information Pro-cessing Systems, pages 6000-6010."}]},{"id":"folio-987545ffb087f1ece898-p11-b11-2-ii","type":"standoff-editor-block","properties":{"standoffProperties":[]},"inline":[{"kind":"text","text":"Pascal Vincent, Hugo Larochelle, Yoshua Bengio, and"}]},{"id":"folio-987545ffb087f1ece898-p11-b13-0-ii","type":"standoff-editor-block","properties":{"standoffProperties":[]},"inline":[{"kind":"text","text":"Pierre-Antoine Manzagol. 2008. Extracting and composing robust features with denoising autoen-coders. In Proceedings of the 25th international conference on Machine learning, pages 1096-1103. ACM."}]},{"id":"folio-987545ffb087f1ece898-p11-b14-0-ii","type":"standoff-editor-block","properties":{"standoffProperties":[]},"inline":[{"kind":"text","text":"Alex Wang, Amanpreet Singh, Julian Michael, Fe-lix Hill, Omer Levy, and Samuel Bowman. 2018a. Glue: A multi-task benchmark and analysis platform"}]},{"id":"folio-987545ffb087f1ece898-p11-b4-0-ii","type":"standoff-editor-block","properties":{"standoffProperties":[]},"inline":[{"kind":"text","text":"4181"}]},{"id":"folio-987545ffb087f1ece898-p12-b0-0-ii","type":"standoff-editor-block","properties":{"standoffProperties":[]},"inline":[{"kind":"text","text":"for natural language understanding. In Proceedings of the 2018 EMNLP Workshop BlackboxNLP: An-alyzing and Interpreting Neural Networks for NLP, pages 353-355."}]},{"id":"folio-987545ffb087f1ece898-p12-b0-1-ii","type":"standoff-editor-block","properties":{"standoffProperties":[]},"inline":[{"kind":"text","text":"Wei Wang, Ming Yan, and Chen Wu. 2018b. Multi-granularity hierarchical attention fusion networks for reading comprehension and question answering. In Proceedings of the 56th Annual Meeting of the As-sociation for Computational Linguistics (Volume 1: Long Papers). Association for Computational Lin-guistics."}]},{"id":"folio-987545ffb087f1ece898-p12-b0-2-ii","type":"standoff-editor-block","properties":{"standoffProperties":[]},"inline":[{"kind":"text","text":"Alex Warstadt, Amanpreet Singh, and Samuel R Bow-man. 2018. Neural network acceptability judg-ments. arXiv preprint arXiv:1805.12471."}]},{"id":"folio-987545ffb087f1ece898-p12-b0-3-ii","type":"standoff-editor-block","properties":{"standoffProperties":[]},"inline":[{"kind":"text","text":"Adina Williams, Nikita Nangia, and Samuel R Bow-man. 2018. A broad-coverage challenge corpus for sentence understanding through inference. In NAACL."}]},{"id":"folio-987545ffb087f1ece898-p12-b0-4-ii","type":"standoff-editor-block","properties":{"standoffProperties":[]},"inline":[{"kind":"text","text":"Yonghui Wu, Mike Schuster, Zhifeng Chen, Quoc V Le, Mohammad Norouzi, Wolfgang Macherey, Maxim Krikun, Yuan Cao, Qin Gao, Klaus Macherey, et al. 2016. Google's neural ma-chine translation system: Bridging the gap between human and machine translation. arXiv preprint arXiv:1609.08144."}]},{"id":"folio-987545ffb087f1ece898-p12-b0-5-ii","type":"standoff-editor-block","properties":{"standoffProperties":[]},"inline":[{"kind":"text","text":"Jason Yosinski, Jeff Clune, Yoshua Bengio, and Hod Lipson. 2014. How transferable are features in deep neural networks? In Advances in neural information processing systems, pages 3320-3328."}]},{"id":"folio-987545ffb087f1ece898-p12-b0-6-ii","type":"standoff-editor-block","properties":{"standoffProperties":[]},"inline":[{"kind":"text","text":"Adams Wei Yu, David Dohan, Minh-Thang Luong, Rui Zhao, Kai Chen, Mohammad Norouzi, and Quoc V Le. 2018. QANet: Combining local convolution with global self-attention for reading comprehen-sion. In ICLR."}]},{"id":"folio-987545ffb087f1ece898-p12-b0-7-ii","type":"standoff-editor-block","properties":{"standoffProperties":[]},"inline":[{"kind":"text","text":"Rowan Zellers, Yonatan Bisk, Roy Schwartz, and Yejin Choi. 2018. Swag: A large-scale adversarial dataset for grounded commonsense inference. In Proceed-ings of the 2018 Conference on Empirical Methods in Natural Language Processing (EMNLP)."}]},{"id":"folio-987545ffb087f1ece898-p12-b0-8-ii","type":"standoff-editor-block","properties":{"standoffProperties":[]},"inline":[{"kind":"text","text":"Yukun Zhu, Ryan Kiros, Rich Zemel, Ruslan Salakhut-dinov, Raquel Urtasun, Antonio Torralba, and Sanja Fidler. 2015. Aligning books and movies: Towards story-like visual explanations by watching movies and reading books. In Proceedings of the IEEE international conference on computer vision, pages 19-27."}]},{"id":"folio-987545ffb087f1ece898-p12-b5-0-ii","type":"standoff-editor-block","properties":{"standoffProperties":[{"id":"folio-987545ffb087f1ece898-p12-b5-0-ii-style/bold-215","type":"style/bold","start":0,"end":34},{"id":"folio-987545ffb087f1ece898-p12-b5-0-ii-style/bold-217","type":"style/bold","start":36,"end":70},{"id":"folio-987545ffb087f1ece898-p12-b5-0-ii-style/bold-221","type":"style/bold","start":72,"end":94}]},"inline":[{"kind":"text","text":"Appendix for \"BERT: Pre-training of Deep Bidirectional Transformers for Language Understanding\""}]},{"id":"folio-987545ffb087f1ece898-p12-b6-0-ii","type":"standoff-editor-block","properties":{"standoffProperties":[]},"inline":[{"kind":"text","text":"We organize the appendix into three sections:"}]},{"id":"folio-987545ffb087f1ece898-p12-b7-0-ii","type":"standoff-editor-block","properties":{"standoffProperties":[]},"inline":[{"kind":"text","text":"• Additional implementation details for BERT are presented in Appendix A;"}]},{"id":"folio-987545ffb087f1ece898-p12-b8-0-ii","type":"standoff-editor-block","properties":{"standoffProperties":[]},"inline":[{"kind":"text","text":"• Additional details for our experiments are presented in Appendix B; and"}]},{"id":"folio-987545ffb087f1ece898-p12-b9-0-ii","type":"standoff-editor-block","properties":{"standoffProperties":[]},"inline":[{"kind":"text","text":"• Additional ablation studies are presented in Appendix C."}]},{"id":"folio-987545ffb087f1ece898-p12-b9-1-ii","type":"standoff-editor-block","properties":{"standoffProperties":[]},"inline":[{"kind":"text","text":"We present additional ablation studies for BERT including:"}]},{"id":"folio-987545ffb087f1ece898-p12-b10-0-ii","type":"standoff-editor-block","properties":{"standoffProperties":[]},"inline":[{"kind":"text","text":"- Effect of Number of Training Steps; and"}]},{"id":"folio-987545ffb087f1ece898-p12-b11-0-ii","type":"standoff-editor-block","properties":{"standoffProperties":[]},"inline":[{"kind":"text","text":"- Ablation for Different Masking Proce-dures."}]},{"id":"folio-987545ffb087f1ece898-p12-b12-0-ii","type":"standoff-editor-block","properties":{"standoffProperties":[{"id":"folio-987545ffb087f1ece898-p12-b12-0-ii-style/bold-48","type":"style/bold","start":0,"end":0},{"id":"folio-987545ffb087f1ece898-p12-b12-0-ii-style/bold-49","type":"style/bold","start":2,"end":28}]},"inline":[{"kind":"text","text":"A Additional Details for BERT"}]},{"id":"folio-987545ffb087f1ece898-p12-b13-0-ii","type":"standoff-editor-block","properties":{"standoffProperties":[{"id":"folio-987545ffb087f1ece898-p12-b13-0-ii-style/bold-53","type":"style/bold","start":0,"end":2},{"id":"folio-987545ffb087f1ece898-p12-b13-0-ii-style/bold-54","type":"style/bold","start":4,"end":41}]},"inline":[{"kind":"text","text":"A.1 Illustration of the Pre-training Tasks"}]},{"id":"folio-987545ffb087f1ece898-p12-b14-0-ii","type":"standoff-editor-block","properties":{"standoffProperties":[]},"inline":[{"kind":"text","text":"We provide examples of the pre-training tasks in the following."}]},{"id":"folio-987545ffb087f1ece898-p12-b14-1-ii","type":"standoff-editor-block","properties":{"standoffProperties":[]},"inline":[{"kind":"text","text":"Masked LM and the Masking Procedure As-suming the unlabeled sentence is my dog is hairy, and during the random masking procedure we chose the 4-th token (which corresponding to hairy), our masking procedure can be further il-lustrated by"}]},{"id":"folio-987545ffb087f1ece898-p12-b15-0-ii","type":"standoff-editor-block","properties":{"standoffProperties":[]},"inline":[{"kind":"text","text":"• 80% of the time: Replace the word with the [MASK] token, e.g., my dog is hairy →"}]},{"id":"folio-987545ffb087f1ece898-p12-b15-1-ii","type":"standoff-editor-block","properties":{"standoffProperties":[]},"inline":[{"kind":"text","text":"my dog is [MASK]"}]},{"id":"folio-987545ffb087f1ece898-p12-b16-0-ii","type":"standoff-editor-block","properties":{"standoffProperties":[]},"inline":[{"kind":"text","text":"• 10% of the time: Replace the word with a random word, e.g., my dog is hairy → my"}]},{"id":"folio-987545ffb087f1ece898-p12-b16-1-ii","type":"standoff-editor-block","properties":{"standoffProperties":[]},"inline":[{"kind":"text","text":"dog is apple"}]},{"id":"folio-987545ffb087f1ece898-p12-b17-0-ii","type":"standoff-editor-block","properties":{"standoffProperties":[]},"inline":[{"kind":"text","text":"• 10% of the time: Keep the word un-changed, e.g., my dog is hairy → my dog is hairy. The purpose of this is to bias the representation towards the actual observed word."}]},{"id":"folio-987545ffb087f1ece898-p12-b17-1-ii","type":"standoff-editor-block","properties":{"standoffProperties":[]},"inline":[{"kind":"text","text":"The advantage of this procedure is that the Transformer encoder does not know which words it will be asked to predict or which have been re-placed by random words, so it is forced to keep a distributional contextual representation of ev-ery input token. Additionally, because random replacement only occurs for 1.5% of all tokens (i.e., 10% of 15%), this does not seem to harm the model's language understanding capability. In Section C.2, we evaluate the impact this proce-dure. Compared to standard langauge model training, the masked LM only make predictions on 15% of tokens in each batch, which suggests that more pre-training steps may be required for the model"}]},{"id":"folio-987545ffb087f1ece898-p12-b7-1-ii","type":"standoff-editor-block","properties":{"standoffProperties":[]},"inline":[{"kind":"text","text":"4182"}]},{"id":"folio-987545ffb087f1ece898-p13-b0-0-ii","type":"standoff-editor-block","properties":{"standoffProperties":[]},"inline":[{"kind":"text","text":"BERT (Ours) OpenAI GPT ELMo T1 T2 ... TN T1 T2 ... TN T1 T2 ... TN Trm Trm ... Trm Trm Trm ... Trm"}]},{"id":"folio-987545ffb087f1ece898-p13-b1-0-ii","type":"standoff-editor-block","properties":{"standoffProperties":[]},"inline":[{"kind":"text","text":"Lstm Lstm ... Lstm Lstm Lstm Lstm"}]},{"id":"folio-987545ffb087f1ece898-p13-b2-0-ii","type":"standoff-editor-block","properties":{"standoffProperties":[]},"inline":[{"kind":"text","text":"..."}]},{"id":"folio-987545ffb087f1ece898-p13-b3-0-ii","type":"standoff-editor-block","properties":{"standoffProperties":[]},"inline":[{"kind":"text","text":"Trm Trm ... Trm Trm Trm ... Trm"}]},{"id":"folio-987545ffb087f1ece898-p13-b4-0-ii","type":"standoff-editor-block","properties":{"standoffProperties":[]},"inline":[{"kind":"text","text":"Lstm Lstm ... Lstm Lstm Lstm ... Lstm"}]},{"id":"folio-987545ffb087f1ece898-p13-b5-0-ii","type":"standoff-editor-block","properties":{"standoffProperties":[]},"inline":[{"kind":"text","text":"E1 E2 ... E E1 E2 ... E N ..."}]},{"id":"folio-987545ffb087f1ece898-p13-b6-0-ii","type":"standoff-editor-block","properties":{"standoffProperties":[]},"inline":[{"kind":"text","text":"N E1 E2 E N"}]},{"id":"folio-987545ffb087f1ece898-p13-b7-0-ii","type":"standoff-editor-block","properties":{"standoffProperties":[]},"inline":[{"kind":"text","text":"Figure 3: Differences in pre-training model architectures. BERT uses a bidirectional Transformer. OpenAI GPT uses a left-to-right Transformer. ELMo uses the concatenation of independently trained left-to-right and right-to-left LSTMs to generate features for downstream tasks. Among the three, only BERT representations are jointly conditioned on both left and right context in all layers. In addition to the architecture differences, BERT and OpenAI GPT are fine-tuning approaches, while ELMo is a feature-based approach."}]},{"id":"folio-987545ffb087f1ece898-p13-b8-0-ii","type":"standoff-editor-block","properties":{"standoffProperties":[]},"inline":[{"kind":"text","text":"to converge. In Section C.1 we demonstrate that MLM does converge marginally slower than a left-to-right model (which predicts every token), but the empirical improvements of the MLM model far outweigh the increased training cost."}]},{"id":"folio-987545ffb087f1ece898-p13-b8-1-ii","type":"standoff-editor-block","properties":{"standoffProperties":[]},"inline":[{"kind":"text","text":"Next Sentence Prediction The next sentence prediction task can be illustrated in the following examples."}]},{"id":"folio-987545ffb087f1ece898-p13-b9-0-ii","type":"standoff-editor-block","properties":{"standoffProperties":[]},"inline":[{"kind":"text","text":"Input ="}]},{"id":"folio-987545ffb087f1ece898-p13-b10-0-ii","type":"standoff-editor-block","properties":{"standoffProperties":[]},"inline":[{"kind":"text","text":"[CLS] the man went to [MASK] store [SEP]"}]},{"id":"folio-987545ffb087f1ece898-p13-b11-0-ii","type":"standoff-editor-block","properties":{"standoffProperties":[]},"inline":[{"kind":"text","text":"he bought a gallon [MASK] milk [SEP]"}]},{"id":"folio-987545ffb087f1ece898-p13-b12-0-ii","type":"standoff-editor-block","properties":{"standoffProperties":[]},"inline":[{"kind":"text","text":"Label ="}]},{"id":"folio-987545ffb087f1ece898-p13-b13-0-ii","type":"standoff-editor-block","properties":{"standoffProperties":[]},"inline":[{"kind":"text","text":"IsNext"}]},{"id":"folio-987545ffb087f1ece898-p13-b14-0-ii","type":"standoff-editor-block","properties":{"standoffProperties":[]},"inline":[{"kind":"text","text":"Input ="}]},{"id":"folio-987545ffb087f1ece898-p13-b15-0-ii","type":"standoff-editor-block","properties":{"standoffProperties":[]},"inline":[{"kind":"text","text":"[CLS] the man [MASK] to the store [SEP]"}]},{"id":"folio-987545ffb087f1ece898-p13-b16-0-ii","type":"standoff-editor-block","properties":{"standoffProperties":[]},"inline":[{"kind":"text","text":"penguin [MASK] are flight ##less birds [SEP]"}]},{"id":"folio-987545ffb087f1ece898-p13-b17-0-ii","type":"standoff-editor-block","properties":{"standoffProperties":[]},"inline":[{"kind":"text","text":"Label ="}]},{"id":"folio-987545ffb087f1ece898-p13-b18-0-ii","type":"standoff-editor-block","properties":{"standoffProperties":[]},"inline":[{"kind":"text","text":"NotNext"}]},{"id":"folio-987545ffb087f1ece898-p13-b19-0-ii","type":"standoff-editor-block","properties":{"standoffProperties":[{"id":"folio-987545ffb087f1ece898-p13-b19-0-ii-style/bold-149","type":"style/bold","start":0,"end":2},{"id":"folio-987545ffb087f1ece898-p13-b19-0-ii-style/bold-150","type":"style/bold","start":4,"end":25}]},"inline":[{"kind":"text","text":"A.2 Pre-training Procedure"}]},{"id":"folio-987545ffb087f1ece898-p13-b20-0-ii","type":"standoff-editor-block","properties":{"standoffProperties":[]},"inline":[{"kind":"text","text":"To generate each training input sequence, we sam-ple two spans of text from the corpus, which we refer to as \"sentences\" even though they are typ-ically much longer than single sentences (but can be shorter also). The first sentence receives the A embedding and the second receives the B embed-ding. 50% of the time B is the actual next sentence that follows A and 50% of the time it is a random sentence, which is done for the \"next sentence pre-diction\" task. They are sampled such that the com-bined length is ≤ 512 tokens. The LM masking is applied after WordPiece tokenization with a uni-form masking rate of 15%, and no special consid-eration given to partial word pieces. We train with batch size of 256 sequences (256 sequences * 512 tokens = 128,000 tokens/batch) for 1,000,000 steps, which is approximately 40"}]},{"id":"folio-987545ffb087f1ece898-p13-b21-0-ii","type":"standoff-editor-block","properties":{"standoffProperties":[]},"inline":[{"kind":"text","text":"epochs over the 3.3 billion word corpus. We use Adam with learning rate of 1e$-4,\\beta _{1}=0.9$, $\\beta _{2}=0.999$, L2 weight decay of 0.01, learning rate warmup over the first 10,000 steps, and linear decay of the learning rate. We use a dropout prob-ability of 0.1 on all layers. We use a gelu acti-vation (Hendrycks and Gimpel, 2016) rather than the standard relu, following OpenAI GPT. The training loss is the sum of the mean masked LM likelihood and the mean next sentence prediction likelihood."}]},{"id":"folio-987545ffb087f1ece898-p13-b22-0-ii","type":"standoff-editor-block","properties":{"standoffProperties":[]},"inline":[{"kind":"text","text":"Training of BERT BASE was performed on 4 Cloud TPUs in Pod configuration (16 TPU chips total).13 Training of BERT LARGE was performed on 16 Cloud TPUs (64 TPU chips total). Each pre-training took 4 days to complete. Longer sequences are disproportionately expen-sive because attention is quadratic to the sequence length. To speed up pretraing in our experiments, we pre-train the model with sequence length of 128 for 90% of the steps. Then, we train the rest 10% of the steps of sequence of 512 to learn the positional embeddings."}]},{"id":"folio-987545ffb087f1ece898-p13-b23-0-ii","type":"standoff-editor-block","properties":{"standoffProperties":[{"id":"folio-987545ffb087f1ece898-p13-b23-0-ii-style/bold-164","type":"style/bold","start":0,"end":2},{"id":"folio-987545ffb087f1ece898-p13-b23-0-ii-style/bold-165","type":"style/bold","start":4,"end":24}]},"inline":[{"kind":"text","text":"A.3 Fine-tuning Procedure"}]},{"id":"folio-987545ffb087f1ece898-p13-b24-0-ii","type":"standoff-editor-block","properties":{"standoffProperties":[]},"inline":[{"kind":"text","text":"For fine-tuning, most model hyperparameters are the same as in pre-training, with the exception of the batch size, learning rate, and number of train-ing epochs. The dropout probability was always kept at 0.1. The optimal hyperparameter values are task-specific, but we found the following range of possible values to work well across all tasks:"}]},{"id":"folio-987545ffb087f1ece898-p13-b25-0-ii","type":"standoff-editor-block","properties":{"standoffProperties":[]},"inline":[{"kind":"text","text":"• Batch size: 16, 32"}]},{"id":"folio-987545ffb087f1ece898-p13-b26-0-ii","type":"standoff-editor-block","properties":{"standoffProperties":[]},"inline":[{"kind":"text","text":"13https://cloudplatform.googleblog.com/2018/06/Cloud-TPU-now-offers-preemptible-pricing-and-global-availability.html"}]},{"id":"folio-987545ffb087f1ece898-p13-b21-1-ii","type":"standoff-editor-block","properties":{"standoffProperties":[]},"inline":[{"kind":"text","text":"4183"}]},{"id":"folio-987545ffb087f1ece898-p14-b0-0-ii","type":"standoff-editor-block","properties":{"standoffProperties":[]},"inline":[{"kind":"text","text":"• Learning rate (Adam): 5e-5, 3e-5, 2e-5"}]},{"id":"folio-987545ffb087f1ece898-p14-b1-0-ii","type":"standoff-editor-block","properties":{"standoffProperties":[]},"inline":[{"kind":"text","text":"• Number of epochs: 2, 3, 4"}]},{"id":"folio-987545ffb087f1ece898-p14-b2-0-ii","type":"standoff-editor-block","properties":{"standoffProperties":[]},"inline":[{"kind":"text","text":"We also observed that large data sets (e.g., 100k+ labeled training examples) were far less sensitive to hyperparameter choice than small data sets. Fine-tuning is typically very fast, so it is rea-sonable to simply run an exhaustive search over the above parameters and choose the model that performs best on the development set."}]},{"id":"folio-987545ffb087f1ece898-p14-b3-0-ii","type":"standoff-editor-block","properties":{"standoffProperties":[{"id":"folio-987545ffb087f1ece898-p14-b3-0-ii-style/bold-35","type":"style/bold","start":0,"end":2},{"id":"folio-987545ffb087f1ece898-p14-b3-0-ii-style/bold-36","type":"style/bold","start":4,"end":32},{"id":"folio-987545ffb087f1ece898-p14-b3-0-ii-style/bold-38","type":"style/bold","start":34,"end":43}]},"inline":[{"kind":"text","text":"A.4 Comparison of BERT, ELMo ,and OpenAI GPT"}]},{"id":"folio-987545ffb087f1ece898-p14-b4-0-ii","type":"standoff-editor-block","properties":{"standoffProperties":[]},"inline":[{"kind":"text","text":"Here we studies the differences in recent popular representation learning models including ELMo, OpenAI GPT and BERT. The comparisons be-tween the model architectures are shown visually in Figure 3. Note that in addition to the architec-ture differences, BERT and OpenAI GPT are fine-tuning approaches, while ELMo is a feature-based approach."}]},{"id":"folio-987545ffb087f1ece898-p14-b5-0-ii","type":"standoff-editor-block","properties":{"standoffProperties":[]},"inline":[{"kind":"text","text":"The most comparable existing pre-training method to BERT is OpenAI GPT, which trains a left-to-right Transformer LM on a large text cor-pus. In fact, many of the design decisions in BERT were intentionally made to make it as close to GPT as possible so that the two methods could be minimally compared. The core argument of this work is that the bi-directionality and the two pre-training tasks presented in Section 3.1 account for the majority of the empirical improvements, but we do note that there are several other differences between how BERT and GPT were trained:"}]},{"id":"folio-987545ffb087f1ece898-p14-b6-0-ii","type":"standoff-editor-block","properties":{"standoffProperties":[]},"inline":[{"kind":"text","text":"• GPT is trained on the BooksCorpus (800M words); BERT is trained on the BooksCor-pus (800M words) and Wikipedia (2,500M words)."}]},{"id":"folio-987545ffb087f1ece898-p14-b7-0-ii","type":"standoff-editor-block","properties":{"standoffProperties":[]},"inline":[{"kind":"text","text":"• GPT uses a sentence separator ([SEP]) and classifier token ([CLS]) which are only in-troduced at fine-tuning time; BERT learns [SEP], [CLS] and sentence A/B embed-dings during pre-training."}]},{"id":"folio-987545ffb087f1ece898-p14-b8-0-ii","type":"standoff-editor-block","properties":{"standoffProperties":[]},"inline":[{"kind":"text","text":"• GPT was trained for 1M steps with a batch size of 32,000 words; BERT was trained for 1M steps with a batch size of 128,000 words."}]},{"id":"folio-987545ffb087f1ece898-p14-b9-0-ii","type":"standoff-editor-block","properties":{"standoffProperties":[]},"inline":[{"kind":"text","text":"• GPT used the same learning rate of 5e-5 for all fine-tuning experiments; BERT chooses a task-specific fine-tuning learning rate which performs the best on the development set."}]},{"id":"folio-987545ffb087f1ece898-p14-b10-0-ii","type":"standoff-editor-block","properties":{"standoffProperties":[]},"inline":[{"kind":"text","text":"To isolate the effect of these differences, we per-form ablation experiments in Section 5.1 which demonstrate that the majority of the improvements are in fact coming from the two pre-training tasks and the bidirectionality they enable."}]},{"id":"folio-987545ffb087f1ece898-p14-b11-0-ii","type":"standoff-editor-block","properties":{"standoffProperties":[{"id":"folio-987545ffb087f1ece898-p14-b11-0-ii-style/bold-24","type":"style/bold","start":0,"end":2},{"id":"folio-987545ffb087f1ece898-p14-b11-0-ii-style/bold-25","type":"style/bold","start":4,"end":44},{"id":"folio-987545ffb087f1ece898-p14-b11-0-ii-style/bold-28","type":"style/bold","start":46,"end":50}]},"inline":[{"kind":"text","text":"A.5 Illustrations of Fine-tuning on Different Tasks"}]},{"id":"folio-987545ffb087f1ece898-p14-b12-0-ii","type":"standoff-editor-block","properties":{"standoffProperties":[]},"inline":[{"kind":"text","text":"The illustration of fine-tuning BERT on different tasks can be seen in Figure 4. Our task-specific models are formed by incorporating BERT with one additional output layer, so a minimal num-ber of parameters need to be learned from scratch. Among the tasks, (a) and (b) are sequence-level tasks while (c) and (d) are token-level tasks. In the figure, E represents the input embedding$,T_{i}$ represents the contextual representation of token i, [CLS] is the special symbol for classification out-put, and [SEP] is the special symbol to separate non-consecutive token sequences."}]},{"id":"folio-987545ffb087f1ece898-p14-b13-0-ii","type":"standoff-editor-block","properties":{"standoffProperties":[{"id":"folio-987545ffb087f1ece898-p14-b13-0-ii-style/bold-66","type":"style/bold","start":0,"end":0},{"id":"folio-987545ffb087f1ece898-p14-b13-0-ii-style/bold-67","type":"style/bold","start":2,"end":28}]},"inline":[{"kind":"text","text":"B Detailed Experimental Setup"}]},{"id":"folio-987545ffb087f1ece898-p14-b14-0-ii","type":"standoff-editor-block","properties":{"standoffProperties":[{"id":"folio-987545ffb087f1ece898-p14-b14-0-ii-style/bold-69","type":"style/bold","start":0,"end":2},{"id":"folio-987545ffb087f1ece898-p14-b14-0-ii-style/bold-70","type":"style/bold","start":4,"end":37},{"id":"folio-987545ffb087f1ece898-p14-b14-0-ii-style/bold-72","type":"style/bold","start":39,"end":60}]},"inline":[{"kind":"text","text":"B.1 Detailed Descriptions for the GLUE Benchmark Experiments."}]},{"id":"folio-987545ffb087f1ece898-p14-b15-0-ii","type":"standoff-editor-block","properties":{"standoffProperties":[]},"inline":[{"kind":"text","text":"The GLUE benchmark includes the following datasets, the descriptions of which were originally summarized in Wang et al. (2018a):"}]},{"id":"folio-987545ffb087f1ece898-p14-b15-1-ii","type":"standoff-editor-block","properties":{"standoffProperties":[]},"inline":[{"kind":"text","text":"MNLI Multi-Genre Natural Language Inference is a large-scale, crowdsourced entailment classifi-cation task (Williams et al., 2018). Given a pair of sentences, the goal is to predict whether the sec-ond sentence is an entailment, contradiction, or neutral with respect to the first one."}]},{"id":"folio-987545ffb087f1ece898-p14-b15-2-ii","type":"standoff-editor-block","properties":{"standoffProperties":[]},"inline":[{"kind":"text","text":"QQP Quora Question Pairs is a binary classifi-cation task where the goal is to determine if two questions asked on Quora are semantically equiv-alent (Chen et al., 2018)."}]},{"id":"folio-987545ffb087f1ece898-p14-b15-3-ii","type":"standoff-editor-block","properties":{"standoffProperties":[]},"inline":[{"kind":"text","text":"QNLI Question Natural Language Inference is a version of the Stanford Question Answering Dataset (Rajpurkar et al., 2016) which has been converted to a binary classification task (Wang et al., 2018a). The positive examples are (ques-tion, sentence) pairs which do contain the correct answer, and the negative examples are (question, sentence) from the same paragraph which do not contain the answer."}]},{"id":"folio-987545ffb087f1ece898-p14-b15-4-ii","type":"standoff-editor-block","properties":{"standoffProperties":[]},"inline":[{"kind":"text","text":"SST-2 The Stanford Sentiment Treebank is a binary single-sentence classification task consist-ing of sentences extracted from movie reviews"}]},{"id":"folio-987545ffb087f1ece898-p14-b9-1-ii","type":"standoff-editor-block","properties":{"standoffProperties":[]},"inline":[{"kind":"text","text":"4184"}]},{"id":"folio-987545ffb087f1ece898-p15-b0-0-ii","type":"standoff-editor-block","properties":{"standoffProperties":[]},"inline":[{"kind":"text","text":"ClassLabel Class Label"}]},{"id":"folio-987545ffb087f1ece898-p15-b1-0-ii","type":"standoff-editor-block","properties":{"standoffProperties":[]},"inline":[{"kind":"text","text":"... ... C T1 T2 ... T N"}]},{"id":"folio-987545ffb087f1ece898-p15-b2-0-ii","type":"standoff-editor-block","properties":{"standoffProperties":[]},"inline":[{"kind":"text","text":"C T1 T N T[SEP] T1' T M'"}]},{"id":"folio-987545ffb087f1ece898-p15-b3-0-ii","type":"standoff-editor-block","properties":{"standoffProperties":[]},"inline":[{"kind":"text","text":"BERT BERT"}]},{"id":"folio-987545ffb087f1ece898-p15-b4-0-ii","type":"standoff-editor-block","properties":{"standoffProperties":[]},"inline":[{"kind":"text","text":"E E1 ... EN E[SEP] E1' ... EM' E[CLS] E1 E2 ... EN"}]},{"id":"folio-987545ffb087f1ece898-p15-b5-0-ii","type":"standoff-editor-block","properties":{"standoffProperties":[]},"inline":[{"kind":"text","text":"[CLS]"}]},{"id":"folio-987545ffb087f1ece898-p15-b6-0-ii","type":"standoff-editor-block","properties":{"standoffProperties":[]},"inline":[{"kind":"text","text":"Tok ... Tok Tok ... Tok [CLS] Tok 1 Tok 2 ... Tok N"}]},{"id":"folio-987545ffb087f1ece898-p15-b7-0-ii","type":"standoff-editor-block","properties":{"standoffProperties":[]},"inline":[{"kind":"text","text":"[CLS] [SEP]"}]},{"id":"folio-987545ffb087f1ece898-p15-b8-0-ii","type":"standoff-editor-block","properties":{"standoffProperties":[]},"inline":[{"kind":"text","text":"1 N 1 M"}]},{"id":"folio-987545ffb087f1ece898-p15-b9-0-ii","type":"standoff-editor-block","properties":{"standoffProperties":[]},"inline":[{"kind":"text","text":"Sentence 1 Sentence 2 Single Sentence"}]},{"id":"folio-987545ffb087f1ece898-p15-b10-0-ii","type":"standoff-editor-block","properties":{"standoffProperties":[]},"inline":[{"kind":"text","text":"Start/End Span O B-PER ... O"}]},{"id":"folio-987545ffb087f1ece898-p15-b11-0-ii","type":"standoff-editor-block","properties":{"standoffProperties":[]},"inline":[{"kind":"text","text":"... ... C T1 T2 ... T N"}]},{"id":"folio-987545ffb087f1ece898-p15-b12-0-ii","type":"standoff-editor-block","properties":{"standoffProperties":[]},"inline":[{"kind":"text","text":"C T1 T N T[SEP] T1' T M'"}]},{"id":"folio-987545ffb087f1ece898-p15-b13-0-ii","type":"standoff-editor-block","properties":{"standoffProperties":[]},"inline":[{"kind":"text","text":"BERT BERT"}]},{"id":"folio-987545ffb087f1ece898-p15-b14-0-ii","type":"standoff-editor-block","properties":{"standoffProperties":[]},"inline":[{"kind":"text","text":"E[CLS] E1 ... EN E[SEP] E1' ... EM' E[CLS] E1 E2 ... EN"}]},{"id":"folio-987545ffb087f1ece898-p15-b15-0-ii","type":"standoff-editor-block","properties":{"standoffProperties":[]},"inline":[{"kind":"text","text":"Tok ... Tok Tok ... Tok [CLS] Tok 1 Tok 2 ... Tok N"}]},{"id":"folio-987545ffb087f1ece898-p15-b16-0-ii","type":"standoff-editor-block","properties":{"standoffProperties":[]},"inline":[{"kind":"text","text":"[CLS] [SEP]"}]},{"id":"folio-987545ffb087f1ece898-p15-b17-0-ii","type":"standoff-editor-block","properties":{"standoffProperties":[]},"inline":[{"kind":"text","text":"1 N 1 M"}]},{"id":"folio-987545ffb087f1ece898-p15-b18-0-ii","type":"standoff-editor-block","properties":{"standoffProperties":[]},"inline":[{"kind":"text","text":"Question Paragraph Single Sentence"}]},{"id":"folio-987545ffb087f1ece898-p15-b19-0-ii","type":"standoff-editor-block","properties":{"standoffProperties":[]},"inline":[{"kind":"text","text":"Figure 4: Illustrations of Fine-tuning BERT on Different Tasks."}]},{"id":"folio-987545ffb087f1ece898-p15-b20-0-ii","type":"standoff-editor-block","properties":{"standoffProperties":[]},"inline":[{"kind":"text","text":"with human annotations of their sentiment (Socher et al., 2013)."}]},{"id":"folio-987545ffb087f1ece898-p15-b21-0-ii","type":"standoff-editor-block","properties":{"standoffProperties":[]},"inline":[{"kind":"text","text":"CoLA The Corpus of Linguistic Acceptability is a binary single-sentence classification task, where the goal is to predict whether an English sentence is linguistically \"acceptable\" or not (Warstadt et al., 2018)."}]},{"id":"folio-987545ffb087f1ece898-p15-b22-0-ii","type":"standoff-editor-block","properties":{"standoffProperties":[]},"inline":[{"kind":"text","text":"STS-B The Semantic Textual Similarity Bench-mark is a collection of sentence pairs drawn from news headlines and other sources (Cer et al., 2017). They were annotated with a score from 1 to 5 denoting how similar the two sentences are in terms of semantic meaning."}]},{"id":"folio-987545ffb087f1ece898-p15-b23-0-ii","type":"standoff-editor-block","properties":{"standoffProperties":[]},"inline":[{"kind":"text","text":"RTE Recognizing Textual Entailment is a bi-nary entailment task similar to MNLI, but with much less training data (Bentivogli et al., 2009).14"}]},{"id":"folio-987545ffb087f1ece898-p15-b24-0-ii","type":"standoff-editor-block","properties":{"standoffProperties":[]},"inline":[{"kind":"text","text":"WNLI Winograd NLI is a small natural lan-guage inference dataset (Levesque et al., 2011). The GLUE webpage notes that there are issues with the construction of this dataset, 15 and every trained system that's been submitted to GLUE has performed worse than the 65.1 baseline accuracy of predicting the majority class. We therefore ex-clude this set to be fair to OpenAI GPT. For our GLUE submission, we always predicted the ma-jority class."}]},{"id":"folio-987545ffb087f1ece898-p15-b25-0-ii","type":"standoff-editor-block","properties":{"standoffProperties":[]},"inline":[{"kind":"text","text":"MRPC Microsoft Research Paraphrase Corpus consists of sentence pairs automatically extracted from online news sources, with human annotations for whether the sentences in the pair are semanti-cally equivalent (Dolan and Brockett, 2005)."}]},{"id":"folio-987545ffb087f1ece898-p15-b26-0-ii","type":"standoff-editor-block","properties":{"standoffProperties":[]},"inline":[{"kind":"text","text":"14Note that we only report single-task fine-tuning results in this paper. A multitask fine-tuning approach could poten-tially push the performance even further. For example, we did observe substantial improvements on RTE from multi-task training with MNLI."}]},{"id":"folio-987545ffb087f1ece898-p15-b27-0-ii","type":"standoff-editor-block","properties":{"standoffProperties":[]},"inline":[{"kind":"text","text":"15https://gluebenchmark.com/faq"}]},{"id":"folio-987545ffb087f1ece898-p15-b28-0-ii","type":"standoff-editor-block","properties":{"standoffProperties":[]},"inline":[{"kind":"text","text":"4185"}]},{"id":"folio-987545ffb087f1ece898-p16-b0-0-ii","type":"standoff-editor-block","properties":{"standoffProperties":[{"id":"folio-987545ffb087f1ece898-p16-b0-0-ii-style/bold-0","type":"style/bold","start":0,"end":0},{"id":"folio-987545ffb087f1ece898-p16-b0-0-ii-style/bold-1","type":"style/bold","start":2,"end":28}]},"inline":[{"kind":"text","text":"C Additional Ablation Studies"}]},{"id":"folio-987545ffb087f1ece898-p16-b1-0-ii","type":"standoff-editor-block","properties":{"standoffProperties":[{"id":"folio-987545ffb087f1ece898-p16-b1-0-ii-style/bold-5","type":"style/bold","start":0,"end":2},{"id":"folio-987545ffb087f1ece898-p16-b1-0-ii-style/bold-6","type":"style/bold","start":4,"end":37}]},"inline":[{"kind":"text","text":"C.1 Effect of Number of Training Steps"}]},{"id":"folio-987545ffb087f1ece898-p16-b2-0-ii","type":"standoff-editor-block","properties":{"standoffProperties":[]},"inline":[{"kind":"text","text":"Figure 5 presents MNLI Dev accuracy after fine-tuning from a checkpoint that has been pre-trained for k steps. This allows us to answer the following questions:"}]},{"id":"folio-987545ffb087f1ece898-p16-b3-0-ii","type":"standoff-editor-block","properties":{"standoffProperties":[]},"inline":[{"kind":"text","text":"1. Question: Does BERT really need such"}]},{"id":"folio-987545ffb087f1ece898-p16-b5-0-ii","type":"standoff-editor-block","properties":{"standoffProperties":[]},"inline":[{"kind":"text","text":"a large amount of pre-training (128,000 words/batch * 1,000,000 steps) to achieve high fine-tuning accuracy?"}]},{"id":"folio-987545ffb087f1ece898-p16-b6-0-ii","type":"standoff-editor-block","properties":{"standoffProperties":[]},"inline":[{"kind":"text","text":"Answer: Yes, BERT BASE achieves almost 1.0% additional accuracy on MNLI when"}]},{"id":"folio-987545ffb087f1ece898-p16-b7-0-ii","type":"standoff-editor-block","properties":{"standoffProperties":[]},"inline":[{"kind":"text","text":"trained on 1M steps compared to 500k steps."}]},{"id":"folio-987545ffb087f1ece898-p16-b8-0-ii","type":"standoff-editor-block","properties":{"standoffProperties":[]},"inline":[{"kind":"text","text":"2. Question: Does MLM pre-training converge"}]},{"id":"folio-987545ffb087f1ece898-p16-b10-0-ii","type":"standoff-editor-block","properties":{"standoffProperties":[]},"inline":[{"kind":"text","text":"slower than LTR pre-training, since only 15% of words are predicted in each batch rather than every word? Answer: The MLM model does converge slightly slower than the LTR model. How-ever, in terms of absolute accuracy the MLM model begins to outperform the LTR model almost immediately."}]},{"id":"folio-987545ffb087f1ece898-p16-b11-0-ii","type":"standoff-editor-block","properties":{"standoffProperties":[{"id":"folio-987545ffb087f1ece898-p16-b11-0-ii-style/bold-117","type":"style/bold","start":0,"end":2},{"id":"folio-987545ffb087f1ece898-p16-b11-0-ii-style/bold-118","type":"style/bold","start":4,"end":33},{"id":"folio-987545ffb087f1ece898-p16-b11-0-ii-style/bold-120","type":"style/bold","start":35,"end":44}]},"inline":[{"kind":"text","text":"C.2 Ablation for Different Masking Procedures"}]},{"id":"folio-987545ffb087f1ece898-p16-b12-0-ii","type":"standoff-editor-block","properties":{"standoffProperties":[]},"inline":[{"kind":"text","text":"In Section 3.1, we mention that BERT uses a mixed strategy for masking the target tokens when pre-training with the masked language model (MLM) objective. The following is an ablation study to evaluate the effect of different masking strategies. Note that the purpose of the masking strategies is to reduce the mismatch between pre-training"}]},{"id":"folio-987545ffb087f1ece898-p16-b14-0-ii","type":"standoff-editor-block","properties":{"standoffProperties":[]},"inline":[{"kind":"text","text":"84"}]},{"id":"folio-987545ffb087f1ece898-p16-b15-0-ii","type":"standoff-editor-block","properties":{"standoffProperties":[]},"inline":[{"kind":"text","text":"82"}]},{"id":"folio-987545ffb087f1ece898-p16-b16-0-ii","type":"standoff-editor-block","properties":{"standoffProperties":[]},"inline":[{"kind":"text","text":"80"}]},{"id":"folio-987545ffb087f1ece898-p16-b17-0-ii","type":"standoff-editor-block","properties":{"standoffProperties":[]},"inline":[{"kind":"text","text":"78"}]},{"id":"folio-987545ffb087f1ece898-p16-b18-0-ii","type":"standoff-editor-block","properties":{"standoffProperties":[]},"inline":[{"kind":"text","text":"BERT BASE (Masked LM) 76 BERT BASE (Left-to-Right)"}]},{"id":"folio-987545ffb087f1ece898-p16-b19-0-ii","type":"standoff-editor-block","properties":{"standoffProperties":[]},"inline":[{"kind":"text","text":"200 400 600 800 1,000"}]},{"id":"folio-987545ffb087f1ece898-p16-b20-0-ii","type":"standoff-editor-block","properties":{"standoffProperties":[]},"inline":[{"kind":"text","text":"Pre-training Steps (Thousands)"}]},{"id":"folio-987545ffb087f1ece898-p16-b21-0-ii","type":"standoff-editor-block","properties":{"standoffProperties":[]},"inline":[{"kind":"text","text":"Figure 5: Ablation over number of training steps. This shows the MNLI accuracy after fine-tuning, starting from model parameters that have been pre-trained for k steps. The x-axis is the value of k."}]},{"id":"folio-987545ffb087f1ece898-p16-b22-0-ii","type":"standoff-editor-block","properties":{"standoffProperties":[]},"inline":[{"kind":"text","text":"and fine-tuning, as the [MASK] symbol never ap-pears during the fine-tuning stage. We report the Dev results for both MNLI and NER. For NER, we report both fine-tuning and feature-based ap-proaches, as we expect the mismatch will be am-plified for the feature-based approach as the model will not have the chance to adjust the representa-tions."}]},{"id":"folio-987545ffb087f1ece898-p16-b23-0-ii","type":"standoff-editor-block","properties":{"standoffProperties":[]},"inline":[{"kind":"text","text":"Masking Rates Dev Set Results"}]},{"id":"folio-987545ffb087f1ece898-p16-b23-1-ii","type":"standoff-editor-block","properties":{"standoffProperties":[]},"inline":[{"kind":"text","text":"MASK SAME RND MNLI NER"}]},{"id":"folio-987545ffb087f1ece898-p16-b24-0-ii","type":"standoff-editor-block","properties":{"standoffProperties":[]},"inline":[{"kind":"text","text":"Fine-tune Fine-tune Feature-based"}]},{"id":"folio-987545ffb087f1ece898-p16-b25-0-ii","type":"standoff-editor-block","properties":{"standoffProperties":[]},"inline":[{"kind":"text","text":"80% 10% 10% 84.2 95.4 94.9 100% 0% 0% 84.3 94.9 94.0 80% 0% 20% 84.1 95.2 94.6 80% 20% 0% 84.4 95.2 94.7 0% 20% 80% 83.7 94.8 94.6 0% 0% 100% 83.6 94.9 94.6"}]},{"id":"folio-987545ffb087f1ece898-p16-b26-0-ii","type":"standoff-editor-block","properties":{"standoffProperties":[]},"inline":[{"kind":"text","text":"Table 8: Ablation over different masking strategies."}]},{"id":"folio-987545ffb087f1ece898-p16-b27-0-ii","type":"standoff-editor-block","properties":{"standoffProperties":[]},"inline":[{"kind":"text","text":"The results are presented in Table 8. In the table, MASK means that we replace the target token with the [MASK] symbol for MLM; SAME means that we keep the target token as is; RND means that we replace the target token with another random token. The numbers in the left part of the table repre-sent the probabilities of the specific strategies used during MLM pre-training (BERT uses 80%, 10%, 10%). The right part of the paper represents the Dev set results. For the feature-based approach, we concatenate the last 4 layers of BERT as the features, which was shown to be the best approach in Section 5.3. From the table it can be seen that fine-tuning is surprisingly robust to different masking strategies. However, as expected, using only the MASK strat-egy was problematic when applying the feature-based approach to NER. Interestingly, using only the RND strategy performs much worse than our strategy as well."}]},{"id":"folio-987545ffb087f1ece898-p16-b28-0-ii","type":"standoff-editor-block","properties":{"standoffProperties":[]},"inline":[{"kind":"text","text":"MNLI Dev Accuracy"}]},{"id":"folio-987545ffb087f1ece898-p16-b22-1-ii","type":"standoff-editor-block","properties":{"standoffProperties":[]},"inline":[{"kind":"text","text":"4186"}]}]},"definitionOwnerBlockIds":[]}
